00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Hey there Shankar here. One of the rewarding aspects of our work at Hidden Brain is that we have the space to experiment with different ways of telling stories. Sometimes we need an hour-long podcast to fully explore a topic. Other times, a short episode can convey our thinking. Today's show is a short essay about one thing our minds do as we confront the COVID-19 pandemic. Over the past few weeks, our TVs have become a time machine. They've transported us to our past to scenes of crowded subway cars and chaotic playgrounds Where faces are unmasked and people said shoulder to shoulder in sports stadiums I have found myself marveling at movies where carefree characters hang out at music clubs It makes me remember a time when I didn't have a thing twice about hugging a friend or stopping by a cafe with a colleague. It's hard to imagine being so carefree again even as many communities
大家好，我是Shankar。在Hidden Brain工作，最令人欣慰的一点就是我们有空间去尝试不同的叙事方式。有时我们需要一个小时的播客来充分探讨一个话题，而有时，一集短小的节目就能传达我们的想法。今天的节目是一篇短文，关于我们在面对新冠疫情时大脑所做的一件事。在过去的几周里，我们的电视机变成了一台时间机器。它们把我们带回过去，带回那些拥挤的地铁车厢和混乱的游乐场，那里的人们没有戴口罩，在体育场里肩并肩坐着。我发现自己惊叹于电影中那些无忧无虑的角色在音乐俱乐部里闲逛的场景。这让我想起了一个我从不担心拥抱朋友或与同事在咖啡馆小憩的时代。即使许多社区正在尝试重新开放，也很难想象还能再次那样无忧无虑。

2
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:53.000
in the U.S. and across the globe make tentative steps to reopen. The ache we feel for a pre-COVID world may seem unproductive. What's the value in looking back at lost freedoms and comforts? Why focus on things we cannot change? Of course, this is how the mind works. Some time ago I was chatting with Clay Rutledge, a psychologist at Nauticoa State University, for a hidden brain episode called The Good Old Days. Clay studies nostalgia and how it affects the way we think about our lives. Many of us experience nostalgia as a bittersweet emotion. It combines the memory of good times
在美国乃至全球，人们都在试探性地迈出重新开放的步伐。我们对疫情前世界的渴望似乎显得毫无成效。回顾失去的自由和舒适有什么价值？为什么要关注我们无法改变的事情？当然，这就是大脑的工作方式。前段时间，我与北达科他州立大学的心理学家Clay Rutledge聊过，那是Hidden Brain一期名为《往日美好时光》的节目。Clay研究怀旧情绪及其如何影响我们对生活的思考。我们许多人将怀旧体验为一种苦乐参半的情感。它结合了美好时光的记忆

3
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:39.000
with the ache of loss. You might think that people who are very nostalgic are more prone to sadness and depression. But Clay found that nostalgic reflection actually makes us optimistic. It reaffirms our social connections. And by remembering important things about the past, it lays out a hopeful vision for the future. I found in recent weeks that my own mind is going back further and further, sometimes to my distant childhood. Last week I cooked a recipe I learned decades ago from a long dead aunt. I remembered how, after placing food before me, she always thought to place some food on her window sill for birds to eat.
与失去的痛苦。你可能认为非常怀旧的人更容易感到悲伤和抑郁。但Clay发现，怀旧的反思实际上让我们变得乐观。它重申了我们的社会联系。通过记住过去重要的事情，它为未来描绘了一幅充满希望的愿景。我发现最近几周，我的思绪回溯得越来越远，有时甚至回到了遥远的童年。上周，我做了一道几十年前从一位早已过世的姑姑那里学来的菜。我记得在把食物端到我面前后，她总是会记得在窗台上放一些食物给鸟儿吃。

4
00:02:39.000 --> 00:03:04.000
When we remember people who have been kind to us, when we remember happy times, we are not merely engaging in fantasy. We are reminding ourselves of our place in the world. Nostalgia can give us renewed appreciation for the people and places that constitute our lives. A number of years ago, Clay surveyed a group of adults in Britain who had been children during World War II.
当我们想起那些善待我们的人，当我们想起快乐的时光时，我们不仅仅是在沉溺于幻想。我们是在提醒自己在这个世界上的位置。怀旧可以让我们重新欣赏那些构成我们生活的人和地方。几年前，Clay调查了一群在二战期间还是孩子的英国成年人。

5
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:44.000
The war was a time of great upheaval for them as their country was bombed and family members were sent after the battlefront. But these people didn't remember the period with horror. They remembered it with nostalgia. They told Clay that it was a time of deep distress, but it was also a moment of profound meaning. It revealed to them what was truly important in their lives, their connections to other people. There was so much movement, so much upheaval, one person told Clay, yet through it all, there is a strong sense of belonging, of security, of family.
战争对他们来说是一个巨大的动荡时期，因为他们的国家遭到轰炸，家庭成员被送往战场。但这些人并没有带着恐惧回忆那段时期，而是带着怀旧之情。他们告诉Clay，那是一段深陷痛苦的时期，但也是一个具有深刻意义的时刻。它向他们揭示了生活中真正重要的东西，即他们与他人的联系。一个人告诉Clay，当时有太多的变动，太多的动荡，但尽管如此，人们心中仍有一种强烈的归属感、安全感和家庭感。

6
00:03:44.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Perhaps you understand this yourself. One thing many of us have realized these past few months is how much we value the people in our lives. Maybe you've had a Zoom happy hour with friends you haven't seen in years. Maybe you've taken long walks with family and noticed new things about your community. Maybe you've found yourself just sitting and doing nothing and connecting with yourself. For those of us who lead frenetic lives, all these can be novel experiences. One useful exercise to practice in these times is to close your eyes and ask yourself what
也许你自己也能理解这一点。过去几个月我们许多人意识到的事情之一，就是我们有多么珍视生命中的人。也许你和多年未见的朋友举行过Zoom欢乐时光。也许你和家人散过长步，并注意到了社区里的新事物。也许你发现自己只是坐着，什么也不做，与自己建立联系。对于我们这些生活节奏极快的人来说，所有这些都可能是新奇的体验。在这些时期，一个有用的练习是闭上眼睛，问问自己

7
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:51.000
you will feel nostalgic about when you look back on the early months of 2020? What will you smile about? What will you miss? Thinking about the present through the eyes of your future self can tell you what is important and beautiful in your life right now. This exercise won't change how hard the pandemic has been for many people. It won't make all the losses disappear. But it will do what the war did for those people in Britain.
当你回首2020年初时，你会对什么感到怀旧？你会对什么微笑？你会怀念什么？通过未来自己的视角来思考现在，可以告诉你此刻生活中什么是重要和美好的。这个练习不会改变疫情对许多人造成的艰难处境，也不会让所有的损失消失。但它会起到战争对那些英国人所起的作用。

8
